{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Compensating Transaction Pattern.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Compensating Transaction Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"compensating transaction pattern compensating transaction pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Compensating Transaction Pattern.md","fileName":"Compensating Transaction Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Compensating Transaction Pattern","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-21 22:12\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Cloud Scalability and Resilience Patterns]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Cloud Scalability and Resilience Patterns","hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Compensating Transaction Pattern.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Compensating Transaction Pattern.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"compensating transaction pattern compensating transaction pattern","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Compensating Transaction Pattern.md","fileName":"Compensating Transaction Pattern.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Compensating Transaction Pattern","heading":"Compensating Transaction Pattern","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A compensating transaction reverses the business effect of a completed step when a later step in a distributed workflow fails. In the book's order example, creating an order is followed by debiting funds; if the debit fails, a new action removes or cancels the order. Compensation is not a database rollback across all services, so its design must specify what happens if the compensating action itself fails or if other observers have already seen the first step.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Cloud Scalability and Resilience Patterns","hands-ondesignpatternswithcandnetcore.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEn